Found it! Look at lines 459–468:

```ts
const frame = (): void => {
  if (this.disposed) return;
  frameMonitor.beginFrame();
  const nowMs = performance.now();
  if (this.rigActive) {
    this.cameraRig.update(Math.min(0.1, (nowMs - this.lastFrameMs) / 1000));
  }
  this.lastFrameMs = nowMs;
  ...
```

`lastFrameMs` — where is it initialized? If it's initialized to `0` (or undefined) in the constructor, and `rigActive` becomes true on the first self ship update, then the first rig update uses `(nowMs - 0)/1000` = a huge dt (e.g., performance.now() since page start = 5000ms → dt clamped to 0.1) — this gets clamped by Math.min(0.1, ...). So the dt is clamped — not NaN. Hmm.

But what if `lastFrameMs` were initialized to undefined… `(nowMs - undefined)/1000 = NaN` → Math.min(0.1, NaN) = **NaN**!! In JS, Math.min(0.1, NaN) = NaN! The clamp does NOT protect against NaN!

Then `cameraRig.update(NaN)`:
```ts
update(dtSec) {
  if (dtSec <= 0) return;   // NaN <= 0 → false, so it continues!
  ...
  const f = 1 - Math.exp(-SMOOTH_K * dtSec);  // NaN
  this.curPos.lerp(targetVec3(target.position), f);  // lerp alpha NaN → curPos = NaN
```

Once curPos is NaN → every subsequent lerp stays NaN → the camera is forever NaN → pure black canvas. Perfect match!

But wait — rigActive only becomes true on the first self-ship update, which happens long after the frame loop has already been running (lastFrameMs gets updated every frame regardless of rigActive — line 468 runs unconditionally). So by the time rigActive=true, lastFrameMs is fresh… unless lastFrameMs is only assigned inside… no, line 468 is outside the if. Hmm, so lastFrameMs should always be fresh.

Unless the frame loop hasn't run even once by the time rigActive becomes true — impossible (self-ship updates arrive ~1s later, and the loop runs every frame).

Let me check the initialization of lastFrameMs. Grep.

